From bob@example.com Thu Feb 26 15:30:00 2026
From: Bob Martinez <bob@example.com>
To: dev@bongoproject.org
Subject: RFC: structured logging conventions
Message-ID: <msg013@example.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Feb 2026 15:30:00 +0000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Team,

We're inconsistent with our slog usage. Proposing these conventions:

1. Always include "component" attribute:
   slog.Info("message", "component", "smtp", ...)

2. Use consistent key names:
   - "user_id" (not "user", "uid", "userId")
   - "message_id" (not "msg_id", "msgId")
   - "duration_ms" (not "elapsed", "took")
   - "error" (not "err", "failure")

3. Log levels:
   - Debug: per-message protocol traces
   - Info: lifecycle events (start, stop, config reload)
   - Warn: recoverable errors, deprecation notices
   - Error: unrecoverable errors that affect a request

4. Never log PII at Info level or above. User emails only at Debug.

Thoughts? I'll write this up as a contributing guide section if
we agree on the approach.

- Bob

From charlie@example.com Fri Feb 27 08:15:00 2026
From: Charlie Wu <charlie@example.com>
To: dev@bongoproject.org
Subject: CalDAV sync issue with Apple Calendar
Message-ID: <msg014@example.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Feb 2026 08:15:00 +0000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Found a compatibility issue: Apple Calendar sends PROPFIND requests
with Depth: infinity on the calendar-home-set, but we only support
Depth: 0 and Depth: 1.

The WebDAV spec (RFC 4918) says servers MAY reject Depth: infinity
with 403, but Apple Calendar treats that as a fatal sync error
instead of falling back.

Fix options:
A) Implement Depth: infinity (more work, spec-complete)
B) Return partial results with Depth: 1 when infinity requested
C) Contact Apple about their client behavior

I'm leaning toward (A) since Google Calendar also sends infinity
in some cases. Going to prototype it today.

- Charlie

From alice@example.com Fri Feb 27 10:00:00 2026
From: Alice Chen <alice@example.com>
To: charlie@example.com
Subject: Re: CalDAV sync issue with Apple Calendar
Message-ID: <msg015@example.com>
In-Reply-To: <msg014@example.com>
References: <msg014@example.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Feb 2026 10:00:00 +0000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Charlie,

Agree on option A. A few things to watch for:

- Depth: infinity can be expensive. Add a max-depth limit (say 5)
  and return 507 Insufficient Storage if exceeded.
- Make sure we don't recurse into other users' calendars if they
  share a collection root.
- Add integration tests using caldav-tester (Apple's test suite).

Also, check if Thunderbird has the same issue -- they've had WebDAV
quirks in the past.

- Alice

